Angular is a modern, TypeScript-based framework maintained by Google for building dynamic, scalable single-page applications (SPAs). It emphasizes a component-driven architecture, two-way data binding, dependency injection, and powerful tools like Angular CLI and DevTools, making it one of the most widely used frameworks for enterprise-grade web apps. 

📘 Brief Note on Angular
Origin: AngularJS (2010) → rewritten as Angular 2+ (2016) for performance and modularity.

Language: Built with TypeScript, offering static typing and better tooling.

Architecture: Component-based, encapsulating HTML, CSS, and logic.

Key Features:

Two-way data binding: Syncs model and view automatically.

Dependency Injection: Promotes modularity and testability.

Directives: Extend HTML with custom behaviors.

Routing: Built-in navigation for SPAs.

Angular CLI: Rapid setup, testing, and deployment.

Server-side rendering (SSR) and lazy loading for performance.

Use Cases: Enterprise dashboards, e-commerce platforms, content management systems, and large-scale SPAs.

📝 25 Angular Interview Q&A
Basics
Q: What is Angular?  
A: Angular is a TypeScript-based open-source framework by Google for building dynamic SPAs with a component-driven architecture.

Q: Difference between AngularJS and Angular?  
A: AngularJS used controllers and scopes; Angular uses components, TypeScript, and improved performance with modularity.

Q: Why use TypeScript in Angular?  
A: TypeScript provides static typing, interfaces, generics, and better tooling, reducing runtime errors.

Q: What is a Component?  
A: A building block in Angular that encapsulates template (HTML), styles (CSS), and logic (TypeScript).

Q: What are Directives?  
A: Special markers in templates that extend HTML behavior (e.g., *ngIf, *ngFor).

Data & Binding
Q: Explain two-way data binding.  
A: Synchronizes data between model and view using [(ngModel)].

Q: Difference between property binding and event binding?  
A: Property binding [ ] passes data to DOM; event binding ( ) listens to events.

Q: What are Pipes in Angular?  
A: Transform displayed data (e.g., date, currency, custom pipes).

Q: What is Reactive Forms vs Template-driven Forms?  
A: Reactive forms use explicit form models in TypeScript; template-driven forms rely on directives in HTML.

Q: What is Change Detection?  
A: Angular’s mechanism to update the view when data changes.

Architecture & Services
Q: What is Dependency Injection (DI)?  
A: A design pattern where services are injected into components, promoting modularity.

Q: What are Angular Modules?  
A: Containers for components, directives, and services (@NgModule).

Q: What is a Service in Angular?  
A: A class that provides reusable logic, often injected via DI.

Q: What is Routing in Angular?  
A: Navigation between views using RouterModule.

Q: What is Lazy Loading?  
A: Loading modules only when needed, improving performance.

Advanced
Q: What is Angular CLI?  
A: Command-line tool for scaffolding, building, testing, and deploying apps.

Q: What is Ahead-of-Time (AOT) Compilation?  
A: Compiles templates at build time, improving performance and reducing runtime errors.

Q: What is Angular Universal?  
A: Enables server-side rendering for better SEO and faster initial load.

Q: What are Guards in Angular Routing?  
A: Functions controlling navigation (e.g., CanActivate, CanDeactivate).

Q: What is Zone.js in Angular?  
A: A library that tracks asynchronous operations for change detection.

Performance & Testing
Q: How does Angular handle testing?  
A: Built-in support with Jasmine, Karma, and TestBed for unit and integration tests.

Q: What is Tree Shaking in Angular?  
A: Removes unused code during build for smaller bundles.

Q: How to optimize Angular performance?  
A: Use lazy loading, AOT, OnPush change detection, and trackBy in *ngFor.

Q: What is RxJS in Angular?  
A: Reactive programming library for handling asynchronous data streams.

Q: What is Ivy Renderer?  
A: Angular’s rendering engine that improves build speed, debugging, and bundle size.
